│  Final Answer:                                                                                           │
│  - Foundation of modern number theory: In the Disquisitiones Arithmeticae (1801) Gauss laid the          │
│  groundwork for the modern theory of integers, introducing systematic use of congruences, quadratic      │
│  residues, Gaussian integers, and laying the groundwork that would lead to later results like the law    │
│  of quadratic reciprocity and powerful techniques in algebraic number theory.                            │
│                                                                                                          │
│  - Theorema Egregium (intrinsic curvature): In 1827 Gauss proved that Gaussian curvature is an           │
│  intrinsic property of a surface, determined entirely by measurements on the surface itself, without     │
│  reference to its embedding in space. This theorem revolutionized differential geometry and the study    │
│  of curved surfaces.                                                                                     │
│                                                                                                          │
│  - Least-squares method and orbital astronomy: Gauss developed and popularized the method of least       │
│  squares for estimating unknown quantities from imperfect data. He used this approach to determine the   │
│  orbit of the asteroid Ceres and to predict its location, enabling its subsequent recovery—a milestone   │
│  in celestial mechanics.                                                                                 │
│                                                                                                          │
│  - Gaussian elimination (a cornerstone of linear algebra): Gauss refined and popularized the             │
│  elimination method for solving systems of linear equations, an algorithm now universally taught as      │
│  Gaussian elimination and foundational to computational linear algebra.                                  │
│                                                                                                          │
│  - Normal distribution and law of errors: Gauss formulated and promoted the law of errors, which         │
│  underpins the normal (Gaussian) distribution. This work established the probabilistic basis for         │
│  measurement errors in science and gave rise to the widespread use of the Gaussian distribution in       │
│  statistics and data analysis.                                                                           │
│                                                                                                          │
