│  Carl Friedrich Gauss reshaped mathematics across number theory, geometry, and statistics. In the        │
│  Disquisitiones Arithmeticae (1801) he laid the groundwork for modern integer theory, introducing        │
│  congruences, quadratic residues, Gaussian integers, and the seeds of quadratic reciprocity and          │
│  algebraic number theory. In differential geometry, his Theorema Egregium showed that Gaussian           │
│  curvature is intrinsic to a surface, independent of its embedding. He popularized the least-squares     │
│  method, using it to compute the asteroid Ceres’s orbit and predict its position. He refined Gaussian    │
│  elimination for solving linear systems and formulated the normal distribution’s law of errors,          │
│  anchoring statistical reasoning and data analysis. For science.                                         │
│                                                                                                          │
